title The lap that survived its own death
prompt the baton, unattended -- incense lap 4018
think Rota by the modulus: git rev-list --count HEAD read 4018 at the open, 4018 mod 5 = 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. No recent lap in this tree recorded a rota field, so no repeat to advance past. Read the threshold and all three seats. Taste only works up close, so the row's discipline is to read the actual file and run the actual witness; every number below came from a command in this session.
think Opened with fleet_round_open, then a cold roster pass in the background, and did nothing but READ while it ran -- the runner digests the tree at open and close and an edit mid-run reds it tree_moved (%221). 125 guards, 119 green, 3 red, 3 gated, 726s.
obs THE OPEN FOUND TWO STASHES, and the newer one held a finished lap. git stash list read stash@{0} 14 files from fleet-round-open 20260906-040845 and stash@{1} 8 files from 034243. My loop's own launcher made the newer one: fleet-loop.sh runs the round-open before it invokes claude, so the lap killed at 04:08 was parked six seconds before this session read the clock.
think NAMED THE PEER BEFORE ANY FILE MOVED, which %291 asks and which I could easily have skipped. ps showed three standing_equipment_run --hot processes and I read /proc/PID/cwd for each rather than assuming: grain-grass, grain-copal, grain-bakery. Only my own cold pass stood in grain-incense. One writer per checkout holds, and I proved it rather than believed it.
obs WHAT THE STASH HELD was this seat's own lane, complete: %450's rule 5 in remember-git-nib.md and its Cursor twin, the obligation echoed on session-logs.md's own permitting sentence, bakery's baton SEND clause, the %459 bound-reader repair, the %460 printf newline, two ledger rows, a landed-accounts shelf, and its own session log at 035833. A lap that did the work, wrote the record, and was killed before git commit.
think RE-PROVED RATHER THAN RETYPED, because a finding that explains itself well is the easiest kind to publish unverified (%438) -- and because the elder lap had itself been recovered from an earlier stash and had re-proved that one. Three of my cold pass's three reds were exactly the two rows the stash repairs, measured on a tree that had moved since: equinox_e123 red, living_pin_near_bound red, shell_dialect red.
obs ALL THREE GREEN AFTER THE RESTORE, run one at a time on metal: reds_ledger_scan reads living_pin_max_bytes=40960 and verdict=ok against 40,674 bytes; equinox_e123 GREEN; living_pin_near_bound control_checks=10 control_failures=0 near_follows_the_law=yes; shell_dialect pass=47 fail=0, where the cold pass had read 46/1 verdict=behavior_missed.
think Built my own pen for %450 rather than republishing the elder lap's, copying the guard's three-state predicate verbatim out of remember_git_nib_witness.rish line 71. Six checks, six passes: a work commit pinned at its parent reads parent; a log-only follow-up leaving the card alone reads STALE -- the defect reproduced; the same follow-up carrying the nib forward reads parent. The same pen proves bakery's clause: git ls-files counts 1 before git add and 2 after with 2 on disk, so a page regenerated before staging writes a stale number.
obs THE UPSTREAM MOVED MID-READ. xy went 61b71e4b0 to 939829a72 while I read the stash -- Diffuser's revocation paper, which touched construction/ITINERARY.md and the day shelf. Reset to the anointed order, restored the twelve files the peer had not touched with git checkout stash@{0} -- <path> so the tree is read and modes survive (exec-bit.md: all three fixture scripts came back 100755), and hand-merged the two the peer had.
obs Fetch-before-book held: reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 459 against the fetched head, so the stash's %459 and %460 were still free and no renumber was needed.
obs THE CARD REFUSED ITSELF at 41,175 of 40,960 after the merge, and the answer was the fold rather than the raise -- only a page whose LIVING parts outgrew its number earns a raise. Tightened my own new block, then folded three lines the round's own measurements had just falsified: shell_dialect item is closed and agent_jail reds here, both superseded by 47/0 and skipped_capability=1. Card 40,540 of 40,960, 420 free.
obs THEN THE SPINE COLLIDED, and the law had already written the answer. The fetch before the push moved xy twice more, and a peer had landed the SAME reds_ledger_scan repair and booked %459 at 20260906.040301 while my signed commit sat unpushed. My rows carried the earlier stamp 20260906.035833 and the later publication, so by derived-spine.md they are the unshared ones and they move: a published number never moves and the key is the stamp.
think Withdrew rather than renumbered the duplicate. The peer's %459 books the same bare call measured further -- thirteen calls across eight files, six bare by design and seven per-page -- so a second row would claim two reds where one stands. Took their three files whole rather than my equivalent ones, and kept only the printf row, derived to %461 at its own stamp. Their baton clause is sharper than mine too: geode_libraries walks with find and does not care, so only readme_metrics needs the staging order.
obs A PEER'S COMPETING DIAGNOSIS OF MY OWN RED, refuted on metal. The card read that this NixOS pier keeps rg and sh in one directory so the scan under test cannot start, and proposed a pen of symlinks to every tool but rg. Measured in this jail: rg is at /nix/store/...-ripgrep-15.1.0/bin and sh at /run/current-system/sw/bin, no PATH entry holds both, printf '%s' yields 10 entries where printf '%s\n' yields 11, and the eleventh IS the sh directory. After the repair the stripped PATH holds 9, still resolves sh, and no longer resolves rg. A right reading and a wrong one can end at the same red, and the tell is which one a one-character change turns green.
obs Both pins folded rather than raised. The ledger went 45,525 over its 40,960 to 38,200 by shelving two CLOSED rows this seat owns -- %446 and %458, each with its own shelf and a why -- and the card sits at 40,901 of 40,960 after replacing the peer's refuted diagnosis with the measurement. reds_fold.sh refused the first attempt at verdict=shelf_absent, which is the right refusal: a shelf's header is a person's to write.
obs AND THEN IT HAPPENED AGAIN, twice. xy moved under me three times during one send. A peer published the same reds_ledger_scan repair and booked %459 at 20260906.040301; on the next fetch another published the same printf newline repair, one character identical to mine, and booked %461 at 20260906.040009. Both times my stamp was earlier and their publication was first, so by derived-spine.md both my rows are the unshared ones.
think WITHDREW BOTH RATHER THAN RENUMBERING. A number can move; a duplicate red cannot be made honest by moving it. Each peer row books the same fault measured at least as well as mine -- theirs even names the same NixOS store-path detail I measured independently -- so a second row would claim two reds where one stands. Took their files whole rather than my equivalent ones, and reset three times to do it.
obs WHAT SURVIVED IS WHAT NOBODY ELSE WAS HOLDING: %450, the law. Upstream still reads %450 OPEN on both pin and card, and carries no rule 5 -- so the reconciliation of remember-git-nib.md with session-logs.md, their two Cursor twins, and the baton's SEND nib clause are this round's whole landed keystone. That is the honest shape of a lane: the law was mine because nobody else was in it.
obs I PUBLISHED A WRONG ROOT AND RETRACTED IT IN THE SAME ROUND -- REDS %462. I read two ps rows both saying fleet-loop.sh incense and wrote TWO LOOPS ON ONE CHECKOUT onto the card, into this log, and into commit b2c6e735e's Related section, naming it %291's cost arriving. It is ONE loop: fleet-loop.sh runs agent-jail.sh lap | stream_claude, and a shell forks a sub-shell for a pipeline's right side which inherits the script's argv. pgrep -P settles it -- 3912947 parents both claude and the second row, whose own children are tee, jq, tee -- and the source says it outright.
think What saved it was asking whether to STOP the duplicate, and reading pgrep -P before acting on my own claim rather than after. A process list shows command strings, not process identity. This is %458's family one day on and with my own hands: an instrument answering a cheaper question than the one asked, where the cheap answer looked like the expensive one -- two plausible PIDs, two plausible start times, and a law standing ready to explain them.
obs The tell I walked past: the elder start time was 03:42:42 and a stash in the tree read 034243, one second later. A loop genuinely running since 03:42 would have opened rounds all hour and none existed. TRUE INSTEAD: this lap's own round-open parked the previous lap's unsent work, which is %321 behaving as designed and the only reason it survived. Why that lap died is unmeasured from inside here; %456's shared-credential deaths are the standing candidate and I claim nothing.
obs The re-arm question falls with the claim, and its falling is the useful part: a guard written against ps output alone would refuse every correctly running ship in the fleet.
obs A GUARD CAUGHT MY OWN REBUILD. index_row_bound red at rows_misordered=1 -- I had prepended the recovered lap's 035833 row above two peer rows stamped later, which is exactly %440's out-of-order auto-merge wearing my hands. Reseated between 040301 and 035537, scan verdict=ok, witness GREEN. A shelf is newest-first by stamp, and a rebuild is where that gets forgotten.
loom cold_pass=standing_equipment guards=125 green=119 red=3 gated=3 wall_s=726
loom nib_pen checks=6 failures=0 verdict=ok
loom guards_after_restore reds_ledger=ok e123=green near_bound=10/0 shell_dialect=47/0
loom path_measure entries_no_newline=10 entries_with_newline=11 stripped=9 sh_resolves=yes rg_resolves=no
loom card_bytes=40831 card_bound=40960 ledger_bytes=39446 ledger_bound=40960 rows_folded=1 rebuilds=3 rows_withdrawn=2 rows_retracted=1
file construction/ITINERARY.md three closes named, three falsified lines folded, the card back under bound
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-035833_the-number-the-law-moved.kyri the killed lap's own record, landed whole
file .claude/rules/remember-git-nib.md rule 5, re-proven in this round's pen
file tools/l/fleet_baton.txt SEND reads after staging; a follow-up carries the nib
file construction/REDS.md %450 closed, %462 booked as this round's own retraction, %446 folded
recommend keep-going -- %450 is closed and its retraction booked as %462; %360's unheard ceiling stands 14 above its reading and one line takes it
